Lab 9: Flavors and Metadata Services
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Define custom flavors (instance types) for different workloads.
Configure metadata services to inject SSH keys and network settings into instances.
Launch instances using custom flavors and metadata.
Prerequisites
A working OpenStack environment (DevStack or a cloud provider with OpenStack API access).
OpenStack CLI (OpenStackClient) installed and configured.
Basic knowledge of Linux command line and YAML/JSON syntax.
Access to a project with admin or member privileges.
Task 1: Define Custom Flavors for Different Workloads
Subtask 1.1: List Existing Flavors
Before creating custom flavors, check the available flavors in your OpenStack environment.

openstack flavor list
Expected Output:
A table showing default flavors (e.g., m1.tiny, m1.small).

Subtask 1.2: Create a Custom Flavor
Define a new flavor optimized for high-CPU workloads (e.g., compute-heavy).

openstack flavor create \
  --vcpus 4 \
  --ram 8192 \
  --disk 20 \
  --public \
  compute-heavy
Explanation:

--vcpus: Number of virtual CPUs.
--ram: Memory in MB.
--disk: Root disk size in GB.
--public: Makes the flavor available to all projects.
Expected Output:
A new flavor named compute-heavy appears in openstack flavor list.

Subtask 1.3: Verify Flavor Creation
openstack flavor show compute-heavy
Expected Output:
Detailed specifications of the compute-heavy flavor.

Task 2: Configure Metadata Services for Instance Configuration
Subtask 2.1: Generate an SSH Key Pair
Metadata services allow injecting SSH keys into instances. First, generate a key pair.

ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ~/.ssh/openstack_lab_key
Explanation:

-t rsa: Uses RSA encryption.
-b 4096: Key length (stronger security).
-f: Saves the key to ~/.ssh/openstack_lab_key.
Expected Output:
Two files created:

openstack_lab_key (private key)
openstack_lab_key.pub (public key)
Subtask 2.2: Add SSH Key to OpenStack
Upload the public key to OpenStack for metadata injection.

openstack keypair create \
  --public-key ~/.ssh/openstack_lab_key.pub \
  lab_keypair
Expected Output:
A new keypair lab_keypair appears in openstack keypair list.

Subtask 2.3: Create Metadata Configuration (User Data)
Metadata can also pass cloud-init configurations (e.g., user setup, packages).

Create a file user-data.yaml:

#cloud-config  
users:  
  - name: labuser  
    ssh-authorized-keys:  
      - ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2E... (paste your public key here)  
    sudo: wheel  
    shell: /bin/bash  
Explanation:

Defines a user labuser with sudo access.
Injects the SSH key for remote access.
Task 3: Launch Instances Using Custom Flavors and Metadata
Subtask 3.1: Launch an Instance with Custom Flavor
Use the compute-heavy flavor and inject metadata.

openstack server create \
  --flavor compute-heavy \
  --image cirros \
  --key-name lab_keypair \
  --user-data user-data.yaml \
  my-custom-instance
Explanation:

--flavor: Uses compute-heavy.
--image: Uses cirros (lightweight test image).
--key-name: Injects the SSH key.
--user-data: Applies cloud-init config.
Expected Output:
A new instance my-custom-instance is created.

Subtask 3.2: Verify Instance Metadata
Check if metadata was applied correctly.

openstack server show my-custom-instance -c key_name -c user_data
Expected Output:
Confirms lab_keypair and user-data.yaml were applied.

Subtask 3.3: Access the Instance via SSH
Use the private key to log in.

chmod 600 ~/.ssh/openstack_lab_key  
ssh -i ~/.ssh/openstack_lab_key labuser@<instance-ip>
Expected Output:
Successful login as labuser.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:
✔ Created custom flavors for specialized workloads.
✔ Configured metadata services to inject SSH keys and user data.
✔ Launched an instance with custom configurations.

These skills are essential for OpenStack administrators and align with Red Hat OpenShift Development I concepts.

Troubleshooting Tips
If SSH fails, check:
Security group rules (allow port 22).
Correct key pair assignment (openstack server show).
If metadata isn’t applied, verify:
Correct YAML syntax in user-data.yaml.
Instance supports cloud-init`.
